{
  "image_name": "0db6e64c712f16f432ea27730f156ba7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how liver macrophages can arise from two sources and then adopt different functional states. On the left, it distinguishes resident Kupffer cells, shown as yolk-sac–derived liver macrophages, from circulating monocytes that come from hematopoietic stem cells. These cells feed into a common macrophage pathway that can polarize in different directions depending on cytokine signals.\n\nOne branch, driven by IL-12 and IFNγ, leads to classically activated M1 macrophages. These are associated with a fibrotic, immune-activating role, including phagocytosis of debris and apoptotic cells and production of pro-inflammatory cytokines. The other branch, driven by IL-4 and IL-13, leads to alternatively activated M2 macrophages, which are linked to profibrotic, immune-suppressive effects such as angiogenesis and anti-inflammatory cytokine production.\n\nA central note lists signaling-related molecules including CSFR1, CCR2, IL4RA, IL13RA1, IL13RA2, and STAT6, suggesting factors involved in this polarization. Between the M1-like and M2-like outcomes, the diagram places a pro-resolution macrophage population marked by CD11b+, F4/80+, and Ly6C low, with dashed arrows implying some plasticity or transition between states. An arrow labeled “targeted modulation?” highlights this pro-resolution state as a possible therapeutic focus.",
